 "grammar": [
  {
   "title": "-m marks the object",
   "rules": [
    "accusative-singular"
   ],
   "body": "In *Puella silvam videt* 'The girl sees the forest', the girl does the seeing – she is the **subject** – and the forest is what she sees, the **object**. Latin marks the object with the ending **-m**: *silva* → *silvam*, *puella* → *puellam*.\n\nThe ending, not the word order, says who does what. *Fēmina puellam vocat* and *Puellam fēmina vocat* both mean 'The woman calls the girl.' To make the girl do the calling, swap the endings: *Puella fēminam vocat*.\n\nThe usual order is subject – object – verb. An adjective takes the same ending as its noun: *silvam magnam* 'a big forest'.\n\nWith *est* there is no object, so no -m: *Silva magna est* 'The forest is big.'",
